{{also|trade-wind}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|trade-wind|tradewind}}

===Etymology===
{{compound+|en|trade|t1=course, path (of running)|pos1=from 14th c.|wind}}, because of their consistent linear direction. Associations with transatlantic trade are a later {{glossary|folk etymology}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹeɪd ˈwɪnd/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪnd|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[steady]] [[wind]] that [[blow]]s from [[east]] to [[west]] above and below the [[equator]].
#: {{syn|en|easterly}}
#: {{ant|en|westerly}}
#: {{ux|en|They rode the '''trade winds''' going west.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Jimmy Cornell|title=World Cruising Routes|publisher=A&C Black|isbn=9780713687774|page=365
|passage=During the summer months, from November to April, the '''trade wind''' is less steady over large parts of the ocean.}}

====Coordinate terms====
* {{l|en|westerly}}

